inger backed by police and business group endorsements plus super PAC support"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a modest Democratic edge: Moskowitz holds a financial advantage and incumbency in a district that leans Republican, while the national environment favors Democrats. Expert ratings average to Tilt D and Polymarket prices Moskowitz around 74%, supporting a narrow but real Democratic margin.", "watch": "Super PAC spending and voter views of Trump could swing this either way; a late polling shift toward Singer would move the race to Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jared Moskowitz (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Jared Moskowitz defends a South Florida district that leans slightly Republican, against former Boca Raton mayor Scott Singer. Moskowitz has raised nearly $3 million and holds a large cash advantage, though Singer has drawn substantial outside support and endorsements from police and business groups. No recent general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals and expert assessments.\n\nRaters are split between Tossup and Lean D, averaging a small Democratic tilt, and prediction markets give Moskowitz roughly a three-in-four chance. The national environment appears favorable to Democrats, but the district's Republican lean keeps this competitive. The race is rated Lean Democratic with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7798, "margin": 4.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7278, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7527, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7814, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.71, "margin": 4.0}]}